  "body": {
    "summary": "BKIND votes No. An audited contract library for Cardano has value, but this action bundles it with three reference implementations, liquid staking among them, asks 53 percent more ada than its own budget needs at today's price, and pays for one year without saying who maintains the library after.",
    "rationaleStatement": "Action: treasury withdrawal 418df5986f50547ec4a709f1a5bce6b850753ad6614c873386b7c953fee84a9f#0, 11,787,063 ada to the 2026 Treasury Reserve Smart Contract named in the proposal. I verified on chain that the action withdraws exactly this amount to that contract under the current constitution's guardrails script, and that the proposal document hashes to the on-chain anchor, 3f0173bf9af7f772002cb18283018e69d780be215c7ddaeafe2e7f9076fdc0fc.\n\nWhat I support. A standardized, audited library that builders import instead of rebuilding and re-auditing the same primitives would cut cost and risk across the ecosystem, and library support for CIP-113, the programmable token-like assets standard merged on 29 September 2026, is the strongest part of the proposal. My No is to the form, not to that work.\n\nOne vote, three purchases. The proposal \"bundles three tightly coupled workstreams into a single integrated engagement\". I cannot fund the library without also funding three reference implementations: a liquid staking protocol, a self-repaying loans protocol and a tokenized money market fund.\n\nThe price. Delivery costs USD 1,831,000; the ada was computed at USD 0.16. At the last Kraken ADA/USD trade when I wrote this, 0.2455 on 1 October 2026, the 11,443,750 ada for delivery are worth USD 2.81 million: 53 percent more than needed. At the last trade before the action was submitted, 0.2268 on 8 September 2026, the margin was already 42 percent. The proposal states neither this margin nor what becomes of the excess beyond \"funds remaining in the contract at expiry sweep back\": not when, and not in what form.\n\nAfter month twelve. The work ends with \"a Year 2 continuation proposal\". No one is named to maintain the library afterwards, no cost is stated, and the next hard fork is not mentioned. An audited library left unmaintained misleads every builder who trusts the audit. The total cost to the treasury is unknown.\n\nConcentrated stake. Ada staking is already liquid; every holder chooses their own pool. The liquid staking blueprint delegates pooled stake \"across an SPO set\" that the proposal does not define, the pattern that on Ethereum let one protocol direct a large share of all stake. My DRep registration commits me to keep power dispersed; I will not fund a template for the opposite.\n\nPromises, not specification. The contract language is still to be chosen, the component list is \"preliminary and indicative\", and 20 percent is paid at signature, before anything is delivered.",
    "precedentDiscussion": "My No votes on actions 154 and 155 both named the same defect: a bundle one ballot cannot separate. This vote is consistent with them.",
    "counterargumentDiscussion": "\"The reference rate is a buffer, and the surplus returns.\" Then state the buffer and the return path. 53 percent is not a rounding choice.\n\nWhat would change my vote: the library and CIP-113 work on their own, priced at a current rate, with a maintenance commitment and its cost beyond month twelve, and without the liquid staking blueprint or with safeguards for how stake is spread across pools.",
    "conclusion": "BKIND votes No: fund the library on its own, at a current price, with its upkeep accounted for (govActionId 418df5986f50547ec4a709f1a5bce6b850753ad6614c873386b7c953fee84a9f#0).",
